        'neighbors' => ['madison', 'janesville', 'fort-atkinson', 'milton', 'edgerton', 'stoughton'],
        'intro' => 'Our Milwaukee office sits on Burleigh Street in Wauwatosa, just west of Highway 100, which puts the whole metro within easy reach: east into the city neighborhoods, west to Brookfield and Waukesha, south through West Allis and Greenfield, north toward Glendale and the shoreline suburbs. When you call (414) 800-9271, you\'re getting a crew that\'s already in the area, not a dispatcher reading your zip code off a screen.',
        'localNotes' => [
            'Milwaukee is a detached-garage town, and anyone who owns one knows exactly what that means. The bungalow and duplex blocks that fill neighborhoods like Washington Heights, Enderis Park, and Story Hill were built in the 1920s with the garage out back on the alley, and those alley garages are where door problems go to marinate: original framing, tight clearances, hardware that predates the safety-reverse era, and nobody\'s heat but the sun\'s. Bay View\'s classic Polish flats tell the same story on the south side. A big share of our city calls are exactly this animal, and we\'ve gotten very good at giving an old alley garage a door that works like a modern one.',
            'The suburbs flip the script. Wauwatosa\'s east side matches the city\'s vintage, but push west past Mayfair and into Brookfield and the housing turns to post-war ranches and 1980s-and-newer colonials with attached two- and three-car garages, insulated doors, and builder-grade openers aging on schedule. Same company, same flat-quote process, different parts bin. We stock the trucks for both because a normal Milwaukee day includes both.',
            'We\'re not a call center with a map. Twins Garage Doors is a family company run by twin brothers, and the Milwaukee metro is served from our own Wauwatosa office, with the same techs, the same pricing discipline, and the same guarantee that built our reputation in Madison: "Done Right, or We Make It Right."',
            'Milwaukee winters are freeze-thaw winters. The lake keeps the city bouncing across the freezing line all season, and every crossing is a cycle: meltwater into the alley ruts and apron cracks by afternoon, ice by midnight. Alley garages take it worst, because the city\'s plows and salt don\'t reach them; the snow gets packed by tires, polished into ice, and frozen against the door bottom. Doors seal themselves to the slab, openers strain against the ice, and the cold snap that follows the thaw is when tired springs give up. If your door lives on an alley, a fresh bottom seal and a fall tension check are the two cheapest things you can do for it.',
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'How much does it cost to repair a garage door in Milwaukee?', 'a' => 'Most spring replacements land between $575 and $1,225 for the complete job, cable repairs between $325 and $625, and minor opener repairs between $100 and $300. Those ranges come from our own completed jobs, not a lead-gen site\'s national table. You approve a flat quote before work starts.'],
            ['q' => 'How quickly can a technician get to me in Milwaukee?', 'a' => 'Same-day in most cases. Our office is on Burleigh Street in Wauwatosa, just west of Highway 100, so the city and the western suburbs are all short runs. Call (414) 800-9271 and we\'ll give you an honest arrival window, not a number we invented.'],
            ['q' => 'Do you work on old alley garages?', 'a' => 'Constantly. Milwaukee\'s 1920s alley garages are a specialty: tight headroom, original framing, and hardware that predates modern safety standards. We can usually fit modern torsion hardware and a safe opener into the space you have, and we\'ll tell you honestly when the framing needs work first.'],
            ['q' => 'Which suburbs do you cover?', 'a' => 'The metro: Wauwatosa, West Allis, Greenfield, Brookfield, Waukesha, New Berlin, Glendale, and the neighboring suburbs. If you\'re not sure you\'re in range, call (414) 800-9271 and ask. The worst case is we tell you honestly that you\'re outside it.'],
            ['q' => 'Can I replace garage door springs myself?', 'a' => 'No. Torsion springs hold hundreds of pounds of tension and injure DIYers every year, and cramped alley garages make the job more dangerous, not less. With the $0 service call with repair, professional diagnosis costs nothing, and the full spring job runs $575 to $1,225.'],
        ],
    ],
